      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability that the first card drawn is a 6 and the second card drawn is a Queen.\n\n1. There are 4 Aces in a standard deck of 52 cards:\n  - The probability of drawing a 6:\n```\nP(Abb) = P(6)\nP(Abb) = 4 / 52\n```\n2. The probability of drawing a Queen:\n```\nP(Cdd) = P(Qd) (Queen of diamonds)\nP(Cdd) = 4 / 52\n```\n3. To find the probability of both events happening, we multiply their individual probabilities:\n```\nP(6 then Queen) = P(Abb) x P(Cdd)\nP(6 then Queen) = (4 / 52) * (4 / 52)\nP(6 then Queen) = 16 / 2704\nP(6 then Queen) ≈ 0.0059\n```\n4. Final answer:\n```\nP(6 then Queen) = 16 / 2704 ≈ 0.0059\n```\n$\\boxed{0.0059}$",
