      "text": "On the first draw, the probability that the deck contains a \"6\" is:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Pr(First Card is a 6)} &= \\frac{4}{52} = \\frac{1}{13}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nAfter the first card is drawn, there are now 51 cards left in the deck.  Thus, the probability that the deck contains a Queen after the first draw is:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Pr(Second Card is a Queen)} &= \\frac{4}{51}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nMultiplying the probabilities of the two individual events using the multiplication rule of independent events, we get:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Pr(First card is a 6 and second card is a Queen)} &= \\text{Pr(First Card is a 6)} \\cdot \\text{Pr(Second Card is a Queen)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{52} \\cdot \\frac{4}{51} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{1}{169}}.\n\\end{align*}",
